"""CS331 Chapter 1 Part 1 (Introduction): explaining notes and explained questions, by slide number.

NOTES replace a slide's plain notes in order (highlights link to note numbers); extra notes go at the end.
Q replaces the deck's old questions: tutorial questions keep their wording, everything else is a prediction.
"""
from .common import pick, truth, tut, tut_truth, tut_written, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The deck builds up in one direction: first what it means for two devices to exchange data, then how many devices are wired together (connections and topologies), then how big a network is (LAN, MAN, WAN), and finally how all those networks join into the Internet through ISPs, and why everyone has to agree on standards for any of it to work.',
        'Tutorial 1 was written from this deck, so its questions show what the instructor checks: definitions (what a protocol is), recognising a mode or topology from a description, counting links with the formulas, and scenario questions where you pick a topology and justify it. Every slide below has those kinds of questions.'],
    2: ['Three sections: **1.1 data communications** (the pieces of one exchange), **1.2 networks** (connecting many devices: connection types, data flow, topology), and **1.3 network types** (LAN, MAN, WAN, the Internet, ISPs and standards). Keep this order in mind, because each section uses the words defined in the one before.'],
    3: ['Data communication is the smallest picture in the course: **two** devices exchanging information over a **transmission medium**. The medium is whatever physically carries the signal: a copper cable, a glass fibre, or radio waves through the air. Everything else in the course (networks, the Internet) is many of these two-device exchanges put together. For it to be useful, the data must arrive at the right device, arrive correctly, and arrive on time.'],
    4: ['Learn the five as answers to five questions. **What** is sent? The message. **Who** sends it? The sender. **Who** gets it? The receiver. **Over what**? The transmission medium. **Following which rules**? The protocol. If an exam option names a device such as a router or switch, it is not one of the five: those are network devices, not parts of the basic model.',
        'The figure draws the protocol box on **both** sides because a protocol only works if both ends follow the same rules. Two people can share a phone line (same medium) and still fail to communicate if one speaks Arabic and the other only Japanese: the medium connected them, but there was no shared protocol. That is the difference between the medium (the path) and the protocol (the agreement).'],
    5: ['A network can only carry bits, so every kind of information is turned into bits first. **Text** is stored as a bit pattern using a code such as **Unicode** (each character gets a number, and the number is sent in binary). **Numbers** are converted directly to **binary**. **Images** are a grid of pixels, and each pixel becomes a bit pattern (1 bit for black and white, more bits for colour). **Audio** is sound, which is continuous, so it is sampled into numbers. **Video** is many images shown quickly one after another (plus audio). The exam point: the form differs, but on the medium it is all bits.'],
    6: ['A **node** is any device on the network. It can be a **host**, a device people actually use (computer, printer, phone), or a **networking device** whose job is to move data (router, switch, modem). A **link** is the pathway between two nodes, and it can be a cable or a wireless channel. So a network is just nodes plus links.',
        'There are only two ways to use a link. **Point-to-point**: the link joins exactly two devices, and its full capacity belongs to them. **Multipoint** (also called multidrop): more than two devices share one link, so they must share its capacity, either at the same time (spatially) or by taking turns (temporally). Sharing is cheaper, but it means devices can get in each other’s way, which is why Chapter 3 needs multiple-access protocols.',
        'A network is judged on three criteria. **Performance** asks how fast: it is measured by **throughput** (how much data actually gets through per second) and **delay** (transit time across the network, and response time to a request). It depends on the number of users, the medium, the hardware and the software. **Reliability** asks how dependable: it is measured by the **frequency of failure** and the **time a link takes to recover** from one. **Security** asks how protected: keeping out unauthorised users, preventing damage to data, and having policies to recover from breaches and data loss. A common trap is mixing up the measures: failure frequency is reliability, not performance.'],
    7: ['**Point-to-point** means the whole capacity of the link is reserved for the two devices on its ends. The figure shows that the link does not have to be a cable: two stations talking through a satellite dish pair are still point-to-point, because nobody else uses that channel. A TV remote and its TV are another everyday example. The word to look for in a question is **dedicated**.'],
    8: ['**Multipoint** means one link, more than two devices. The figure shows a mainframe and three workstations hanging off a single cable. Because they share it, only so much capacity is available: either they divide it so several transmit at once (**spatial** sharing), or they take turns using all of it (**temporal**, time sharing). If a question says “more than two devices share a single link”, the answer is multipoint.'],
    9: ['**Simplex**: data only ever travels in **one** direction, like a one-way street. One device only sends and the other only receives. Examples: a keyboard sending to a computer, a monitor receiving from it, a radio broadcast. The receiver can never answer on that channel.',
        '**Half-duplex**: both devices can send, but **not at the same time**. They take turns, and whoever is sending gets the **entire** capacity of the channel. A walkie-talkie is the standard example: you press the button, speak, say “over”, and only then can the other person reply. If both press at once, neither gets through.',
        '**Full-duplex**: both devices send and receive **at the same time**, like a phone call where both people can talk at once. The catch is that the channel’s capacity is **shared between the two directions** all the time, either because the link has two separate paths (one per direction) or because the capacity is divided. Tutorial 1 asks exactly this: “capacity shared by both directions at all times” means full-duplex.'],
    10: ['**Topology** is the shape of the network: how the devices and links are **physically** arranged, or how devices are interconnected. It matters because the shape decides how much cable you need, what happens when something breaks, and how easy it is to add a device. Each topology on the next slides is a different trade-off between cost and robustness.',
         'There are four basic topologies: **mesh** (everyone linked to everyone), **star** (everyone linked to a centre), **bus** (everyone on one shared cable) and **ring** (everyone linked to two neighbours in a loop). Real networks often combine them, which is called a **hybrid** topology (slide 15).'],
    11: ['In a fully connected mesh every device has its own dedicated link to every other device. To count the links: each of the N devices connects to the other N-1, which gives N(N-1), but that counts every link twice (A to B and B to A are the same cable), so divide by 2: **N(N-1)/2**. Each device needs one I/O port (one NIC) per link it has, so **N-1 ports per device**. Example: 5 devices need 5 x 4 / 2 = 10 links and 4 ports each.',
         'Why is mesh **expensive**? Because the number of cables and ports grows roughly with the square of the number of devices: 10 devices need 45 links, 50 devices need 1,225. Every link is a cable to buy, install and maintain, which is also why it is hard to install and update. What you get for that cost: dedicated links mean no traffic problems and better privacy (nobody else sees your link), a broken link only affects the two devices on it, and faults are easy to find. That is why mesh is used where reliability matters most, such as backbone links between main routers.'],
    12: ['In a star, each device has a dedicated point-to-point link **only to the central controller** (a hub or switch), never directly to another device. To send from A to B, the data goes A to the hub, then hub to B. That is why it needs only **N links** for N devices, one per device, and each device needs only one port. It is cheaper than mesh, easy to install, and adding or removing a device means plugging one cable into the hub.',
         'Star is called **robust** because if one cable breaks, only the device on that cable is cut off; everyone else keeps working, and the broken link is easy to identify. But the whole network depends on the centre: **if the hub or switch fails, nothing works**. That is called a single point of failure. In exam answers, mention both: robust against link failures, not against hub failure.'],
    13: ['A bus is one long cable, called the **backbone** or main cable, running past all the devices. Each device connects to it through a short **drop line** attached with a **tap**. So for N devices you need **1 main cable + N drop lines**. Signals travel along the whole backbone and every device hears every transmission. The ends of the cable need terminators so the signal doesn’t bounce back. Bus needs less cable than mesh or star because the backbone can take the most efficient route.',
         'The weakness is the shared backbone. **A single break in the main cable stops all transmission**, because the signal can no longer pass and it reflects off the break. Faults are **hard to isolate** since one cable serves everybody, and **adding devices is hard** because each tap weakens the signal and the backbone has a limited length. This is why the old shared-cable Ethernet LANs were replaced by star (switched) LANs.'],
    14: ['In a ring, each device has a dedicated point-to-point link to **exactly two neighbours**, one on each side, forming a closed loop, so N devices need **N links**. Data travels around the ring in one direction, passing from device to device. Each device contains a **repeater** that regenerates the signal and passes it on if the data isn’t addressed to it. Faults are easy to find: if a device stops getting a signal within the expected time, it raises an alarm, so the break is located quickly.',
         'Ring is **not robust**: since data must travel around the loop, **one broken link or one failed device can stop the whole ring** (in a simple, one-direction ring). The usual fix is a **dual ring**, a second loop that runs in the other direction and takes over when the first one breaks. Compare with star: in a star, a broken cable cuts off one device; in a ring, it can cut off everyone.'],
    15: ['A **hybrid** topology combines two or more basic ones. The figure shows a **star backbone**: a central device connects several bus segments (star-bus) or several rings (star-ring). This is how real organisations grow: each department may have its own small network, and a central device ties them together. In an exam, if a description mixes two shapes, the answer is hybrid.'],
    16: ['Networks are also classified by **geographic size**. A **LAN** (local area network) covers a room, a building or a campus, up to about a kilometre, and is usually privately owned. A **MAN** (metropolitan area network) covers a town or city, around 10 km. A **WAN** (wide area network) covers a country, a continent or the whole world. The figure’s table runs from 1 m (a personal area network) to 10,000 km (the Internet).',
         'Tutorial 1 tests this with a true/false: “A LAN typically covers an area larger than 2 miles” is **false**. A LAN is limited to a few kilometres at most, so if a network spans a whole city it is a MAN, and between cities or countries it is a WAN.'],
    17: ['The figure contrasts two ways of building the same LAN. **In the past**, all hosts were attached to **one shared cable**, which is a bus: only one host could send at a time, and a cable break took down everyone. **Today**, each host has its **own cable to a switch**, which is a star: the switch forwards each frame only to the host it is meant for, so hosts no longer compete for one cable. This shift (shared bus to switched star) comes back in Chapter 4 on Ethernet.'],
    18: ['A **MAN** covers a town or a city. It is larger than a LAN but smaller than a WAN, and it usually connects many LANs across the city through a public network run by a telecom company (the slide’s example is STC). A typical use: a company with several branches in Riyadh linking their LANs through a city-wide network, or a cable TV network that also provides Internet across a city.'],
    19: ['This figure shows how a real campus LAN is built in layers. Each building has its own switches connecting the PCs (a Gigabit LAN). Those building switches connect to a faster central **backbone switch** (10 Gigabit), because the backbone carries everyone’s traffic combined. A single **router** connects the whole LAN to the Internet. The idea to take away: switches connect devices within a LAN, and a router connects the LAN to other networks.'],
    20: ['An **internetwork**, or **internet** with a small i, is what you get when **two or more networks are connected**, using routers at the joins. In the figure, a LAN on the west coast and a LAN on the east coast are joined by routers R1 and R2 over a point-to-point WAN link, so together they form a small internet.',
         'The **Internet** with a capital I is one particular internet: the worldwide one we all use, running TCP/IP. So every Internet is an internet, but not every internet is the Internet. Tutorial 1’s true/false says they are the same, which is **false**.'],
    21: ['Real networks are **heterogeneous**: they mix different kinds of network. Here a service provider’s network (routers connected by WAN links) connects several LANs and a home user. Wherever two networks meet, there is a **router**, because a router is the device that moves data between networks. Recognise the pattern: LANs at the edges, WAN links between them, routers at every boundary.'],
    22: ['A **WAN** provides **long-distance** transmission: between cities, countries and continents. LANs are usually owned by the organisation using them, while WAN links are usually leased from telecom companies. WANs are often built as a mesh (or partial mesh) of point-to-point links between sites, because a mesh gives each pair of sites its own link and keeps working when one link fails. Tutorial 1’s first scenario (offices in New York, London, Tokyo and Sydney) is a WAN question.'],
    23: ['The **Internet** is a collection of many different networks, LANs and WANs, joined by connecting devices and switching stations, that all run the **TCP/IP** protocol suite. TCP/IP is what makes it one Internet rather than many separate networks: it is the common language every connected network agrees to speak.',
         'For a host to count as being **on the Internet**, it needs three things: it must **run TCP/IP**, it must have an **Internet (IP) address** so others can reach it, and it must be able to **send IP packets** to other machines on the Internet. A computer on a private network with no IP route out is not on the Internet, even if it runs TCP/IP internally.'],
    24: ['ISPs are arranged in tiers. **Tier 1** ISPs are the **backbones** of the Internet: international, very few of them, they **own their own links and routers** across continents, and they **connect directly to each other**, so they never pay anyone to reach the rest of the Internet. Examples: Sprint, AT&T, Lumen.',
         '**Tier 2** ISPs are **provider networks** with national or regional coverage. They are a country’s gateway to the international Internet, and each one is **connected to at least one tier-1 ISP**. STC, ITC and Mobily are the Saudi examples.',
         '**Tier 3** ISPs are **local ISPs**. They don’t have international links of their own; they **take service from the national providers** and sell access to homes and businesses. So traffic from your laptop typically climbs tier 3, tier 2, tier 1 and back down to reach a server abroad.'],
    25: ['This map shows what makes a tier-1 ISP: Lumen owns links across the oceans and on every continent. Because it owns this infrastructure, it can reach any other network by connecting to the other tier-1 backbones, without buying transit from anyone.'],
    26: ['The Internet is a **network of networks**. Tier-1 ISPs sit in the centre and connect to each other; tier-2 ISPs connect to tier 1; local and tier-3 ISPs connect to tier 2 and are its customers. The **access network** is the last hop: the link that connects the end systems (your home router, your phone) to their local ISP. No single organisation owns the whole thing.'],
    27: ['This is the same structure drawn another way. The **backbones** (tier 1) are in the middle and are joined at **peering points**. The **provider networks** (tier 2) connect to the backbones, and the **customer networks** (homes, companies) connect to the providers at the edge. Read it from bottom to top as the path your data takes to leave your country.'],
    28: ['**Peering** is the interconnection of two networks so they can exchange traffic directly. Without peering, two ISPs could only reach each other through some third network. The slide gives two ways to peer.',
         '**Private peering** is a direct connection between ISPs. The peering points are a group of routers **owned and operated by the ISPs themselves**, not by a third-party company. An **Internet Exchange Point (IXP)** is public peering: a physical facility **managed by a third party**, built from high-speed switching networks, where **any ISP can connect with any other**. IXPs carry enormous amounts of traffic.',
         'The current trend: **international ISPs** (tier 1) connect to each other at **private peering points**, while **national ISPs** interconnect using **IXPs**. The trap in exams is who runs what: private peering points are run by the ISPs; an IXP is run by a third party.'],
    29: ['Why an IXP matters: without one, a customer of ISP-A in Riyadh sending data to a customer of ISP-B in the same city might have their traffic travel abroad and back, through transit providers, which is slow and costs money. With an IXP, ISP-A and ISP-B exchange that traffic locally. The Saudi Arabian Internet Exchange is the local example.'],
    30: ['In the early 1970s, IBM, Digital Equipment and other vendors each built their own networking architecture that only worked with **their own** computers. A company with IBM machines and DEC machines could not connect them without major **translation software**. The problem this created is the reason standards exist: devices from different makers have to agree on common rules.',
         'A **protocol** is a **set of rules** that controls how data is exchanged between two entities. An **entity** is anything that can send and receive information, such as an application (an email program, a browser). A **system** is the physical machine it runs on (a computer, a terminal, a remote sensor). Tutorial 1’s trap: “A protocol is the structure or format of the data” is **false**, because format (syntax) is only one part of a protocol; a protocol also covers meaning (semantics) and timing.'],
    31: ['**Standards** are agreed protocols published so that everyone can follow them. They specify the **hardware and software** procedures so computers can talk to each other **correctly** and **reliably**, and their main purpose is **interoperability**: equipment from different vendors working together. Without standards, a Dell laptop could only talk to a Dell router.',
         'The benefits: standards create a **large market** (a vendor can sell to everyone, which lowers prices), give **guidelines** to manufacturers, vendors and government agencies, and let **products from different vendors communicate**. The list at the bottom shows how many separate things must be agreed: how many volts mean 0 or 1, where a message ends, what to do with lost messages, how big each data type is, how machines are identified, how to find a route to a machine, and how applications talk. Each of these questions becomes a job for one layer in Part 2.'],
    32: ['Know each organisation by what it covers. **ISO** is the international body (it made the OSI model). **ITU-T** is the United Nations agency for telecommunications standards. **ANSI** is the United States national standards body. **IEEE** focuses on **LAN and MAN** standards, for example 802.3 (Ethernet) and 802.11 (Wi-Fi). **W3C** sets the standards for the World Wide Web (HTML, CSS). If a question says LAN or MAN standards, the answer is IEEE.'],
}

